tmux reduced to tabs. no prefix key, no panes, no copy mode.
for running coding agents and ssh sessions side by side.

    ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ 1 shell   2 codex   3 pi  [4 claude]     ^T new  ^W close  ^← ^→ │
    ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
    │ ╭─ Claude Code ────────────────────────────────────────────────╮ │
    │ │                                                              │ │
    │ │                     Welcome back Yahya!                      │ │
    │ │                                                              │ │
    │ │                           ▐▛███▜▌                            │ │
    │ │                          ▝▜█████▛▘                           │ │
    │ │                            ▘▘ ▝▝                             │ │
    │ │                                                              │ │
    │ │               Opus 5 (1M context) with xhig…                 │ │
    │ │                         Claude Max                           │ │
    │ │                            ~/dev                             │ │
    │ │                                                              │ │
    │ ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ │
    │                                                                  │
    │ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
    │ ❯ Try "how does <filepath> work?"                                │
    │ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
    │   ⏵⏵ bypass permissions on (shift+tab to cycle) · ← for agents   │
    └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

keys:

    ctrl+t        new tab (claude / codex / pi / opencode / shell)
    ctrl+w        close tab
    ctrl+→ ←      next / previous tab

requires bash and tmux ≥ 3.0. install:

    $ curl -o ~/.local/bin/tabs https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sueszli/tmux-tabs/master/tmux-tabs.sh
    $ chmod +x ~/.local/bin/tabs
    $ tabs

closing the terminal leaves everything running; `tabs` reattaches.
a tab closes when its process exits. runs on its own tmux socket and
never loads ~/.tmux.conf. works in stock macOS terminal and any linux
terminal without configuration.

ctrl+tab is not bindable: terminals send it as a plain tab.
